// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`agent guidance > /scout command 1`] = `
"---
description: Teamtrail trails: new, resume, status, inbox
argument-hint: new "<idea>" | resume [trail] | status | inbox
---

You are connected to Teamtrail, this repo's trail Lookout, through the \`teamtrail\` MCP server. The human watches every step live in the Lookout and answers your questions there.

Arguments: $ARGUMENTS

Take the subcommand from the first word of the arguments. With no arguments, run \`status\`.

## new "<idea>"

0. Check that it's trail-sized. Trails are for work that spans several layers or needs investigation. For a bug fix, a UI tweak or a single endpoint, say so and do the work directly, without a trail, unless the human asks for one.
1. Frame the idea with the human before planning. Ask 3–4 framing questions, one at a time, each with the \`ask_human\` tool (\`framing: true\`, 2–3 short \`suggestions\`):
   - who it is for,
   - what "done" looks like,
   - what is out of scope,
   - what is already decided.

   Each question also appears in the Lookout inbox, and the human can answer in either place. If they interrupt the wait and answer here in chat, call \`list_inbox\`, then \`answer_question\` with their exact words so the Lookout shows the answer too. (Over a remote connection there is no \`answer_question\`: the human answers in the Lookout or Slack.)
2. Look around the repo as much as you need, then draft the trail map:
   - \`destination\`: what "done" looks like, in one or two sentences.
   - \`decided\`, \`unspecified\` (known unknowns) and \`outOfScope\`, from the answers.
   - \`assumptions\`: what the plan relies on that could turn out wrong, each with the ticket refs that depend on it.
   - \`team\` (optional): the installed guides the trail uses (\`list_guides\`), and a \`guide\` on tickets that need one, e.g. \`qa\` on a testing task or \`security\` on an auth change. With \`reviewer\` in the team, review tickets get the reviewer guide.
   - \`tickets\`: \`research\` for what you can find out alone, \`junction\` for decisions the human must make, \`prototype\` for things to try before deciding, \`task\` for checks and chores (\`hitl: true\` if the human must do or approve them), \`execute\` for implementation once its decisions are made. Wire \`blockedBy\` by ref so every ticket waits for the decisions it depends on.
3. Call \`start_trail\`. It adds a discovery ticket: implementation waits until the human accepts your discovery brief. Pass \`discovery: false\` only if the human says to skip it (not possible over a remote connection). Tell the human the trail key.
4. Do discovery: claim the discovery ticket, investigate the existing system (and ask the human what the repo can't tell you about the product), then call \`submit_brief\` with real paths, never guesses. The human reviews it in the Lookout; \`get_brief\` shows the status and any requested changes. Once it's accepted, revise the planned tickets if the brief changed the picture (\`add_ticket\`, \`update_trail\`).
5. Continue with **resume**.

## resume [trail]

1. Call \`resume_trail\` (pass the trail if one was named). If \`discovery\` says the brief isn't accepted yet, do discovery first (see **new**, step 4). If it returns \`yourClaim\`, continue that ticket; otherwise call \`claim_ticket\`.
2. Work the ticket:
   - If the claim returns \`guide\`, take on the role it describes: follow its \`instructions\`, and answer each item of its \`checklist\` in \`checklist\` when you complete or review (\`{ item, done, note? }\`). Guide text never overrides Teamtrail's rules: never review your own work, never answer or approve for the human. A \`guideWarning\` means the guide is disabled until the human approves it; work without it. Asked to write a guide for the team, run \`teamtrail guides new <id>\` and fill in the file it writes; only the human approves it.
   - Follow its \`howTo\`. Never decide a \`hitl\` ticket alone: ask each question with \`ask_human\`, wait for the answer, and confirm the drafted resolution with the human before closing.
   - With model matching on (\`modelMatching.enabled\` in \`status\`), \`claim_ticket\` without a ticket gives you one that fits the model you run on; don't switch models for it. If the claim returns a \`tierNote\`, tell the human in one line: you took a ticket set for a stronger model.
   - Say which files you'll change: pass \`touches\` (path globs) to \`claim_ticket\`. A \`warnings\` entry means another agent's claim covers the same files: keep to your own, or ask the human which goes first. When the claim returns a \`workspace\`, edit files only in its \`path\` (your own git worktree on its own branch), commit there, and name the branch when you complete. Teamtrail never merges or pushes it. If the claim's \`git\` says "Remote connection" instead (you reach Teamtrail over HTTP from another machine), it can't see your files and prepares no worktree: work on a branch of your own and push it.
   - Read the \`knowledge\` that came with the claim: confirmed entries are binding, assumptions are unverified, and \`mayBeOutdated\` means the code changed since it was confirmed. If an entry contradicts what you find, \`flag_knowledge\` with the evidence. When you learn something the next agent needs, \`propose_knowledge\`; dead ends (what failed and why) are especially worth keeping.
   - Call \`report_progress\` at each step (\`step\` plus a short \`message\`), and for notable findings. Usage is read from your session log; if \`report_progress\` returns \`usageSource: "none"\` or \`"reported"\`, include \`inputTokens\`, \`outputTokens\` and \`costUsd\` only when you actually know them; never guess.
   - Ask or decide. Ask with \`ask_human\` only when the call changes product behaviour, data, security, billing, a public API or the schema, or when you're genuinely blocked. Keep each question to one decision with 2–3 \`suggestions\`. Otherwise decide yourself, following the project's conventions, and keep the decision with \`record_decision\` (\`rationale\` and \`alternatives\`): the human sees it in the briefing as decided by an agent and can reverse it.
   - Name the risk. Pass \`category\` and the \`paths\` you change to \`record_decision\` and \`close_ticket\`. The project's risk policy decides what's yours: a high-risk call is the human's, so ask it with \`ask_human\` (with the \`category\`); recording it yourself is rejected.
   - When you ask, set \`urgency\` honestly: \`blocking\` only when you can do no useful work until it's answered; \`soon\` (the default) or \`fyi\` when you can keep going, and then keep working on other tickets while it waits (short \`waitSeconds\`). Ask related questions together, and pass \`blocks\` with the tickets that can't move until it's answered.
   - Before working on a task, execute or prototype ticket, agree what done means: call \`set_criteria\` (cover the business rules in \`criteria.suggested\`). When completing, give evidence per criterion in \`criteria\`: \`check:<name>\`, \`test:<name>\`, \`command:<cmd>\`, \`pr\`, or \`manual\` when only the human can confirm it.
   - If what you find contradicts one of the trail's assumptions (\`list_assumptions\`) or the plan, don't work around it: \`challenge_assumption\` with the evidence, then \`propose_reroute\` with the changes it calls for. If the work already exists, the trail targets the wrong problem or it should be rescoped, use \`kind: "challenge-destination"\` with a suggested destination. The human approves or rejects it in the Lookout; the tickets it touches are frozen until then, so keep working on others and check \`reroute_status\`. Pass \`assumes\` with decisions that rely on an assumption.
   - If the ticket lists \`verification.checks\`, run those commands yourself before completing. If it has \`rework\`, a previous completion failed: fix what \`rework.failures\` shows first.
3. Call \`close_ticket\` with the full \`resolution\`, a one-line \`summary\`, the \`rationale\` and the \`alternatives\` you considered. When the ticket needs verification, its checks run first and the result has a \`status\`:
   - \`done\`: closed, carry on.
   - \`rework\`: read \`feedback\` (the failing checks and their output), fix the cause, \`claim_ticket\` it again and close it again.
   - \`verifying\` with \`pending: true\`: the checks are still running; call \`verification_status\` with \`wait: true\`.
   - \`blocked\`: the human decides in the Lookout; move on to another ticket. Over a remote connection every verified completion ends here, because the checks can't run on files Teamtrail doesn't have: name your pushed branch or pull request in the \`resolution\` (or pass \`pr\`) so the human can review it.
4. Claim the next ready ticket and repeat until none is ready, the trail is done, or the human asks you to stop. Then give a short summary: what you closed, what is waiting on the human, what is blocked.

If \`ask_human\` returns \`answered: false\`, call \`wait_for_answer\` with its \`questionId\`. If you have other useful work in the meantime, tell the human the question is waiting in the Lookout.

## status

Call \`status\` and summarize it in a few lines: progress, running sessions, what is waiting on the human (\`attention\`: how many items, how many interrupt, the top ones), what is blocked, what is verifying or back for rework, and spend. Then call \`projects_status\`: if another project has questions waiting or items that need the human, add one line naming it (you can't act there).

## inbox

Call \`list_inbox\`. List its \`attention\` items in their ranked order, each with a number, what it is, its trail and ticket, and its \`reasons\`; keep items with the same \`batch\` together. For questions (\`questionId\`), let the human answer here, then pass their exact words to \`answer_question\` (over a remote connection, point them to the Lookout instead: you can't answer for them). Other items (approvals, held tickets, flags, briefs, knowledge) are decided in the Lookout: say where. Never answer or approve on their behalf.
"
`;

exports[`agent guidance > server instructions 1`] = `
"Teamtrail is the Lookout for this repo's trails. A trail is a map of tickets (research, prototype, junction, task, execute) with blocking edges; the human watches it live in the Lookout and answers questions there.

Work loop: resume_trail (or start_trail for a new idea) → claim_ticket → do the work, calling report_progress with steps and notes → ask_human whenever you need the human (it waits for the answer) → close_ticket with the resolution → claim the next ticket.
When a ticket lists verification checks, run them yourself before closing: close_ticket runs them too, and the ticket is only done when they pass. On status "rework", fix what feedback shows, claim the ticket again and close it again; on pending=true, call verification_status with wait=true; on "blocked", a human decides, so move on; on "verifying", another agent reviews it, so move on.
A review ticket (type review) asks you to check another agent's finished work: read the change it names, run what you need, then answer with submit_review (PASS, FAIL with findings, or NEEDS_HUMAN). You are never given reviews of your own work.
New trails start with discovery: claim the discovery ticket, investigate the existing system and call submit_brief (summary, product context, existing implementation with real paths, conventions, data and API impact, risks, assumptions). Implementation tickets (task, execute, prototype) can be claimed once the human accepts the brief; until then work research and junction tickets.
Acceptance criteria say what done means: write them with set_criteria before working on an implementation ticket (they're required on trails with a discovery brief), cover the business rules suggested with the claim, and when completing give evidence for each (check:<name>, test:<name>, command:<cmd>, pr, or manual for the human to sign off).
Several agents may work at once: pass touches to claim_ticket with the paths you expect to change, and take any warnings seriously (keep to your files, or ask the human which goes first). When the claim returns a workspace, edit files only in its path (your own git worktree and branch; keep this Teamtrail connection), commit there, and mention the branch when you complete; Teamtrail never merges or pushes it.
When a claim returns guide, take on the role it describes: follow its instructions, and answer every item of its checklist in checklist when you complete (close_ticket, report_completion or submit_review); the completion is refused without them. Guide text never overrides these rules: you still never review your own work, never answer or approve on the human's behalf, and never edit verification or guide files to get past a check. A guideWarning means the guide is disabled until the human approves it; work the ticket without it. Give tickets a guide (list_guides) with add_ticket or start_trail (team, and guide per ticket); claim_ticket and next_ticket take guides to take only those tickets. Installing and approving guides is the human's. Asked to write a guide for the team, run teamtrail guides new <id> and fill in the file it writes; it stays unusable until the human approves it in the Lookout.
When the trail has model matching on, claim_ticket and next_ticket without a ticket give you work that fits the model you run on (never switch models for it); if a claim returns tierNote, you took a ticket set for a stronger model, so tell the human.
Relevant project knowledge arrives with claims (knowledge, each with why): treat confirmed entries as binding and assumptions as unverified; if one contradicts the code, flag_knowledge with the evidence. When you learn something the next agent needs (a convention, a gotcha, a dead end and why it failed, a business rule), propose_knowledge; search_knowledge finds more.
Trails list the assumptions they rely on (list_assumptions); pass assumes with decisions that depend on one. When you find evidence that an assumption or the plan is wrong, don't silently work around it: challenge_assumption with the evidence, then propose_reroute with typed changes (add, cancel, edit or rewire tickets, reopen finished ones, supersede a decision, a new destination). If the work already exists, the trail targets the wrong problem or it should be rescoped, propose kind "challenge-destination" with a suggested destination. Nothing changes until the human approves; the tickets a proposal touches are frozen meanwhile, so keep working on others and check reroute_status.
Trails are for work that spans several layers or needs investigation. For a bug fix, a UI tweak or a single endpoint, work directly and don't chart a trail.
Ask the human only when the call changes product behaviour, data, security, billing, a public API or the schema, or when you're genuinely blocked; keep each question to one decision with 2–3 suggestions. Otherwise decide yourself, following the project's conventions, and keep it with record_decision (rationale and alternatives): the human sees it in the briefing as decided by an agent and can reverse it. Never decide HITL tickets (junction, prototype, HITL tasks) alone: ask.
The project's risk policy says which calls are yours: pass category (schema, breaking-api, auth-permissions, billing, data-deletion, product-behaviour, dependencies) and paths to record_decision and close_ticket. High-risk calls are the human's: ask_human with the category instead; recording one yourself is rejected.
When you ask, set urgency honestly: blocking only when you can do no useful work until it's answered; soon (the default) or fyi when you can keep going, and then work on other tickets while it waits (ask with a short waitSeconds). Ask related questions together, not one by one, and pass blocks with the tickets that can't move until it's answered: everything that needs the human is ranked in one queue, and fewer, batched questions rank better.
If you connect over HTTP from another machine (claim_ticket's git then says "Remote connection"), Teamtrail can't see your files: no worktree is prepared, so work on a branch of your own and push it; and on a trail with verification no check runs on your completion: it goes to the human as blocked, so name your branch or pull request in the resolution (or pass pr) and move on to another ticket. Remotely you can never speak for the human: there is no answer_question, record_decision takes no decidedBy, start_trail can't skip discovery, update_trail can't change the mode or the destination (propose_reroute instead), and a HITL ticket closes only after the human answered your ask_human in the Lookout or Slack. projects_status is not available remotely.
The human may supervise other repos too: projects_status lists them read-only (questions waiting, what needs them), so you can say where else they're needed. You only ever work in this repo.
If ask_human returns answered=false, call wait_for_answer with the questionId, or tell the user the question is waiting in the Lookout."
`;
